# ⚠ THIS FILE HOSTS TWO TESTS AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS A GAP. Read both before
# concluding anything about it.
#
# ── THE RUST LANE: FIXED (R49 Track A2-α, `t0389` closed) ──
# Calling a `Callable[...]`-typed LOCAL VARIABLE with a consuming argument used
# to panic the Rust lowerer on a program `gg check` accepted:
#
#   thread 'gg-main' panicked at src/ir/lowering/mod.rs:2154:
#   Tier 2a consume-site violation: 1 violation(s) in module '<unknown>'.
#   First: fn @main bb0 i5 — CollectionMutator(__gorget_closure_call_3,
#   arg #0) — untracked source consumed (ownership not decided).
#
# ⭐ THE ICE WAS A FALSE POSITIVE MANUFACTURED BY THE CALLEE'S OWN NAME.
# `is_consume_extern` asks `module.consume_externs`, a set derived at module
# finalization from `fn_param_ownerships` by taking every function with an
# `Ownership::Move` parameter. The closure-call arm had INJECTED the callable's
# ownerships into `fn_param_ownerships` under its manufactured
# `__gorget_closure_call_<slot>` key — purely so `lower_call_arg` could read
# them back — so finalization duly derived the SYNTHETIC name into
# `consume_externs`, and the validator demanded a decided ownership for an
# argument no arm had registered. A compiler-invented callee was classified as
# a collection mutator because a table keyed by a made-up name said so. With
# the callee's identity on `Instruction::CallIndirect` there is no key, no
# derived entry and no violation.
#
# Asserted LIVE by `callable_local_var_consuming_arg` on both Rust backends;
# ASan-clean under `detect_leaks=1`.
#
# ── THE SELF-HOST LANE: STILL A GAP (`todo/t1118`) ──
# The self-host emits C that compiles clean and then exits 139.
#
# ⚠ `t0389` RECORDED THE OPPOSITE — "PANICS Rust gg while the SELF-HOST prints
# correctly", a *reference lags the self-host* cell. That half has DECAYED.
# Re-measured 2026-09-04 with a driver built from PRISTINE committed source, so
# it is PRE-EXISTING: A2-α neither caused it nor fixed it. Asserted by the
# `#[ignore]`d `sh_callable_local_var_consuming_arg`, which is why this file
# stays under `known_gaps/` even though its Rust test is live — a live test is
# invisible to `scripts/known_gaps_census.sh`, so without that row nothing
# would ever notice the self-host half being fixed.
#
# ⚠ AND IT IS NOT `t1055`. That item is the self-host's indirect-callee NAME
# decode firing on a USER function's spelling. Here the callee genuinely IS a
# closure, so the prefix decode is doing its job. Different mechanism.
#
# ── WHY IT STAYS IN `known_gaps/` RATHER THAN MOVING TOP-LEVEL ──
# `tests/fixtures/*.gg` is auto-scanned into `runtime_parity_corpus`, which
# gates the self-host lane. Moving this file would enrol a self-host SEGV there
# — raising the runtime-diff non-match count for a track's own inflow, which is
# exactly what a track may not do. It moves the round `t1118` closes.
#
# ── SHAPE NOTES (still current) ──
# GLYPH-INDEPENDENT: the retired-glyph twin (`String !s` in the signature,
# `Callable[void(int &, String !)]` in the type, `f(&a, !greeting)` at the call)
# panicked with the BYTE-IDENTICAL message, so this was never a D27 issue.
#
# DISCRIMINATOR vs the working case: the same program with an INDEXED callee
# always compiled and ran on the Rust lane —
#   Vector[Callable[void(int &, String ^)]] arr = []
#   arr.push(mix)
#   arr[0](&a, ^greeting)
# — which is `callable_bang_arr_indexed_callee.gg`, the one in-corpus cell of
# that shape. That is what identified the hole as specific to calling a
# Callable-typed LOCAL BINDING. (`d27_sh_caret_fntype_param_suffix.gg` is NOT a
# caret twin of the indexed shape: it passes its callable as a PARAMETER and
# shares only the fn-type param-suffix parse position. The indexed shape also
# runs on the Rust lane ONLY — it times out on the self-host runtime lane,
# filed separately.)
#
# Expected stdout on BOTH lanes: hi\n101

void mix(int &n, String ^s):
    n = n + 100
    print(s)

void main():
    int a = 1
    String greeting = "hi"
    Callable[void(int &, String ^)] f = mix
    f(&a, ^greeting)
    print(a)
